          { name: "Neglecting Non-College-Educated Working-Class Constituents as a Coherent Voting Bloc", tag: "avoid", why: "At 23.9% college attainment and $76,300 median household income, NE-LD-32's working-class and non-college-educated majority is the core constituency—and in rural districts this group is increasingly electorally volatile when they perceive their representatives as addressing professional-class or urban concerns rather than their own material lives. With Democratic opposition absent, there is no partisan mechanism to mobilize this constituency against an incumbent, but the absence of opposition should not breed complacency about their actual economic concerns and satisfaction. A challenger who speaks directly to working-class economic anxiety, healthcare access, and whether government is delivering tangible results can activate this constituency even in a deeply Republican district if the incumbent has become visibly inattentive to their material wellbeing." }
